Problems found
==============

1. No error handling  
   • Network failures (offline, DNS, CORS, etc.) will throw and be left un-handled.  
   • A non-2xx/3xx HTTP status (404, 500, 401 …) is treated as success and will make response.json() run on a body that may be empty or contain an error message.  
   • response.json() itself can throw if the body is not valid JSON (e.g. 204 No Content).

2. Unsanitised/Unvalidated id  
   • The function accepts anything and interpolates it directly into the URL.  
   • Characters such as “/”, “?”, “#”, “&”, “\n” or “..” can change the request path and make the browser hit a different endpoint.  
   • If id is supplied by an attacker this can be used for request-smuggling, log-pollution or for probing internal APIs.  
   • Type confusion (passing an object) may generate “/api/users/[object Object]”.

3. No timeout / abort logic  
   fetch() will wait indefinitely unless the server or browser closes the
   connection. A hung request ties up the tab / worker.

4. Missing security & interoperability headers  
   If the server returns JSON but forgets the correct MIME type the client
   fails.  Accept: application/json should be sent.  
   If authentication cookies are required, credentials: "include" (or "same-origin") should be set.

5. Information leakage through GET  
   GET requests end up in browser history, proxies and server logs.  If the
   user id is sensitive, a POST with a JSON body or an Authorization header
   would be safer.

6. Ambiguous return type for the caller  
   The function returns a promise but does not tell the caller whether it
   resolved with “user data” or “an error object”.  A small wrapper that
   normalises success/error simplifies the call-site.

7. No type checking / documentation  
   In TS/JS projects it is easy to pass the wrong thing and discover the bug
   only at run-time.

How to fix it
=============

```javascript
/**
 * Fetch a user record.
 * @param {string|number} id – Numeric ID only.
 * @param {Object}  [options]
 * @param {number}  [options.timeout=8000] – Abort after n ms.
 * @returns {Promise<Object>} The parsed user object.
 * @throws {TypeError|Error}  Normalised error on failure.
 */
export async function getUser(id, { timeout = 8000 } = {}) {
  // 1. Validate/encode id
  if (!/^\d+$/.test(String(id))) {
    throw new TypeError('id must be a positive integer');
  }
  const url = `/api/users/${encodeURIComponent(id)}`;

  // 2. Optional timeout / AbortController
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timer = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), timeout);

  try {
    const res = await fetch(url, {
      method: 'GET',
      headers: { 'Accept': 'application/json' },
      credentials: 'same-origin',          // or "include" if cross-site cookies are needed
      signal: controller.signal
    });
    clearTimeout(timer);

    // 3. Handle non-OK status codes
    if (!res.ok) {
      const text = await res.text().catch(() => '');
      throw new Error(`GET ${url} failed: ${res.status} ${res.statusText}${text ? ' – ' + text : ''}`);
    }

    // 4. Parse JSON safely
    try {
      return await res.json();
    } catch (e) {
      throw new Error(`Server returned invalid JSON for ${url}`);
    }
  } catch (e) {
    if (e.name === 'AbortError') e.message = `Request timed out after ${timeout} ms`;
    throw e;
  }
}
```

What this improves
------------------
• Robust – all error paths are caught and surfaced as regular JS errors.  
• Resistant – id is validated and URI-encoded, blocking injection tricks.  
• Responsive – hung servers won’t freeze the UI; AbortController bails out.  
• Secure – correct headers and credential policy, optional switch to POST if
  the identifier is sensitive.  
• Predictable – caller always receives either a user object or a thrown
  Error, never “undefined behaviour”.
